<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carignan, Ardennes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ivoix, Yvois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carignan is a small town on the Chiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, known until 1662 as Ivoix or Yvois. A Roman settlement and medieval fortified town, it belonged to the County of Chiny and then to Luxembourg, and changed hands repeatedly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and France. After it passed to France in 1659, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> gave it to Eugene Maurice of Savoy, prince of Carignano, after whose title it was renamed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carignan-ardennes.jpg" alt="Carignan, Ardennes" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szeder László · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FR-08-Carignan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
